WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:02.734
-Thank you for coming back
on the show.

00:00:02.734 --> 00:00:03.901
-Thanks for having me, man.
-You know, I love having you.

00:00:03.901 --> 00:00:05.300
And every time you come on,

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:07.300
you always bring it
with your performance.

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:09.501
And tonight is the --
it's fantastic.

00:00:09.501 --> 00:00:10.801
-I'm excited. Yeah.
-I loved it.

00:00:10.801 --> 00:00:12.667
I was watching rehearsal.
I'm like -- I was gonna run over

00:00:12.667 --> 00:00:13.934
and just be like,
"Dude, I love it!"

00:00:13.934 --> 00:00:15.100
But, like, no,
you were in the zone.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:16.400
It was great, bud.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:18.467
Thank you so much
for coming back on the show.

00:00:18.467 --> 00:00:19.601
I want to talk to you about this

00:00:19.601 --> 00:00:21.701
'cause it went kind of crazy
on the Internet.

00:00:21.701 --> 00:00:24.000
You had a recent run-in
on your -- on a flight.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.334
-[ Laughs ] Yeah.
Yeah.

00:00:25.334 --> 00:00:27.067
-You were going from -- where?
From L.A. to Chicago?

00:00:27.067 --> 00:00:30.000
-Yeah. So, I was in L.A.,
shooting for my new show,

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.734
"The Voice."
-Oh, yeah!

00:00:31.734 --> 00:00:33.067
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:33.067 --> 00:00:35.834
-And I've been out there
for about a month-and-a-half.

00:00:35.834 --> 00:00:38.667
And me and my oldest daughter,
we were headed back to Chicago

00:00:38.667 --> 00:00:41.200
and got on this flight
and, you know, in a rush.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:42.801
And we get on the plane,
we realize that our seats

00:00:42.801 --> 00:00:44.567
aren't together.
And so, I'm kind of, like,

00:00:44.567 --> 00:00:46.834
doing the awkward dad,
standing around, like,

00:00:46.834 --> 00:00:50.000
not trying to ask everybody,
but kind of like, you know --

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:51.801
But before I could --
-"Can we shuffle these seats?"

00:00:51.801 --> 00:00:53.300
Yeah, I want to sit
next to my daughter," yeah.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:55.200
-But before I could even, like,
make the suggestion,

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:58.033
this kind older gentleman
gets up, and he's like,

00:00:58.033 --> 00:00:59.334
"The girl can have my seat."

00:00:59.334 --> 00:01:02.667
And I looked him in his face,
and I'm like,

00:01:02.667 --> 00:01:04.400
"Are you Martin Short?"

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:06.000
[ Laughter ]

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.400
And it was Martin Short!
It was the Martin Short!

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.234
It was the real Martin Short.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:10.234 --> 00:01:12.000
-I love that dude!
-That was so cool.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.968
He's amazing.
-I love that dude!

00:01:13.968 --> 00:01:15.467
-No, I love him, too!
My daughter loves him

00:01:15.467 --> 00:01:17.100
'cause he's in
"The Santa Clause 3,"

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:18.367
the best --
[ Laughter ]

00:01:18.367 --> 00:01:21.167
The best Santa Claus
in the "Santa Clause" franchise.

00:01:21.167 --> 00:01:22.501
-The best in the franchise.
-Yeah.

00:01:22.501 --> 00:01:25.234
-Did you -- did you freak out
a little bit?

00:01:25.234 --> 00:01:27.834
-I -- I super freaked out.
I almost told him

00:01:27.834 --> 00:01:29.467
that it was fine and that
I would just sit next to him.

00:01:29.467 --> 00:01:31.667
Kensli would be --
[ Laughter ]

00:01:31.667 --> 00:01:33.467
-Yeah, "I was --
I want to sit next to you

00:01:33.467 --> 00:01:34.801
and talk to you all flight."
-No, man, it was cool.

00:01:34.801 --> 00:01:36.100
He was really, really nice.

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:38.434
And I don't think
he knows who I am.

00:01:38.434 --> 00:01:40.634
So, I think he just did it --

00:01:40.634 --> 00:01:42.434
Which is hilarious, but also,
it's like,

00:01:42.434 --> 00:01:43.868
"That's a really kind gesture,"
you know what I mean?

00:01:43.868 --> 00:01:45.033
-Yeah, he's a good dude.
-Yeah.

00:01:45.033 --> 00:01:46.367
-He's good like that.

00:01:46.367 --> 00:01:49.000
Are you a talker on a flight,
or are you just quiet?

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:50.300
-If I'm sitting next
to Martin Short,

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:52.200
I'd be a talker, for sure.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.133
I got scripts, man.
Like, I'll --

00:01:54.133 --> 00:01:55.534
[ Laughter ]

00:01:55.534 --> 00:01:59.634
-I remember I was on a flight
with Mandy Patinkin,

00:01:59.634 --> 00:02:02.167
and he was --
I just found him so interesting.

00:02:02.167 --> 00:02:04.300
And I was -- and I couldn't
stop talking to him.

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:05.434
And I could tell
he was getting annoyed.

00:02:05.434 --> 00:02:06.868
[ Laughter ]

00:02:06.868 --> 00:02:08.467
He goes like --
But I was, like, talking about

00:02:08.467 --> 00:02:10.567
"SNL" days and Belushi,
and all of sudden, he was like,

00:02:10.567 --> 00:02:12.200
"What? What?"
[ Laughter ]

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:13.534
"What's it --"
Like, he wasn't behind me.

00:02:13.534 --> 00:02:14.667
He didn't even
have headphones in.

00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:16.534
He was just doing this
with his ear.

00:02:16.534 --> 00:02:18.601
Yeah, he did not want
to talk to me at all.

00:02:18.601 --> 00:02:20.434
I also saw this tweet,

00:02:20.434 --> 00:02:22.234
and I would like a little
explanation on this.

00:02:22.234 --> 00:02:23.734
-You do follow me.
-Okay -- I do.

00:02:23.734 --> 00:02:24.901
[ Laughter ]

00:02:24.901 --> 00:02:27.467
"Day one of trying to get
Target and Pizza Hut

00:02:27.467 --> 00:02:28.634
to squash the beef."

00:02:28.634 --> 00:02:29.767
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:29.767 --> 00:02:31.234
-What are you --
What are you talking about?

00:02:31.234 --> 00:02:33.300
-I can't be the only person
that's noticing this.

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.501
Do you guys not remember a time
where you used to go to Target

00:02:35.501 --> 00:02:37.701
and you would have a cart
in one hand

00:02:37.701 --> 00:02:40.434
and a personal pep pizza
in the other hand?

00:02:40.434 --> 00:02:41.901
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:41.901 --> 00:02:43.400
What's going on, Target?

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:45.701
Like, I just feel like they --
I don't know.

00:02:45.701 --> 00:02:47.133
There's, like,
a silent beef going on.

00:02:47.133 --> 00:02:48.501
I just would like them to --

00:02:48.501 --> 00:02:49.901
And I have no problem
with Starbucks, too.

00:02:49.901 --> 00:02:51.434
I think that they're great.
-Oh, it's Starbucks now.

00:02:51.434 --> 00:02:53.033
-Yeah. They have, like --
They got, like,

00:02:53.033 --> 00:02:55.434
croissants and hot drinks.
And I love Starbucks, again.

00:02:55.434 --> 00:02:58.234
'Cause I don't -- you know.
-But when you're shopping

00:02:58.234 --> 00:03:00.868
at Target, you want your pep.
-Yeah, and I just can't --

00:03:00.868 --> 00:03:03.267
I find -- it's just
a little empty feeling

00:03:03.267 --> 00:03:05.300
when you go there and you can't.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:07.100
-By the way, how much fun
is Target, by the way?

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:08.400
I --
-Target's amazing.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:09.868
-I didn't know anything about
Target until, like,

00:03:09.868 --> 00:03:11.000
a couple years ago,
and someone's like,

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:12.734
"Dude, you gotta go.
It's a whole deal."

00:03:12.734 --> 00:03:14.367
-No, Target's got --
they've got everything.

00:03:14.367 --> 00:03:16.167
They've got household
appliances.

00:03:16.167 --> 00:03:18.000
They got produce.
-They got a D.J.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.033
-They got the DVD --
They got a D.J.?

00:03:20.033 --> 00:03:21.367
-I don't know. Someone --
-Oh, yeah.

00:03:21.367 --> 00:03:22.801
[ Laughter ]
Sounds like the club now.

00:03:22.801 --> 00:03:24.033
-They've got great music
playing.

00:03:24.033 --> 00:03:25.067
I was, like,
dancing down the aisle.

00:03:25.067 --> 00:03:26.467
I'm like --
-No, it's fire.

00:03:26.467 --> 00:03:28.167
-I loved it.
I didn't want to leave Target.

00:03:28.167 --> 00:03:30.200
-Yeah. Before I give them
all this free promotion...

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:31.400
[ Laughter ]
-Sorry, no, yeah.

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:33.334
Not a paid ad.
No, yeah, yeah.

00:03:33.334 --> 00:03:34.434
They got --
Other stores are good, too.

00:03:34.434 --> 00:03:36.234
Yeah. I love --
[ Laughter ]

00:03:36.234 --> 00:03:38.701
I love Kohl's.
I love Kohl's.

00:03:38.701 --> 00:03:40.234
I love -- yeah, yeah.

00:03:40.234 --> 00:03:42.334
I love all those.
And Domino's pizza is good, too.

00:03:42.334 --> 00:03:43.601
Yeah, yeah, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:43.601 --> 00:03:44.667
Yeah, we don't work
for either one.

00:03:44.667 --> 00:03:46.334
-It's great.
-Last time you were on,

00:03:46.334 --> 00:03:48.601
we were talking about this
Black Star Line Festival...

00:03:48.601 --> 00:03:51.033
-Yeah.
-...that you put on in Ghana.

00:03:51.033 --> 00:03:53.467
-In Ghana, in West Africa, yes.
-You were -- you were like,

00:03:53.467 --> 00:03:54.801
"Dude, I don't know if
I'm gonna pull this off,

00:03:54.801 --> 00:03:56.300
or what's gonna go down."

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:58.234
Did you pull it off?
-We -- yeah, we --

00:03:58.234 --> 00:04:01.534
It was very successful.
The Black Star Line Fest.

00:04:01.534 --> 00:04:03.367
-Look at this.
That -- how many people --

00:04:03.367 --> 00:04:05.434
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:05.434 --> 00:04:07.234
How many people showed up?
-No, it was magical.

00:04:07.234 --> 00:04:10.868
50,000 people --
52,000 people.

00:04:10.868 --> 00:04:12.267
-Showed up.
-Largest --

00:04:12.267 --> 00:04:14.000
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:16.834
-Largest concert
in the history of Ghana.

00:04:16.834 --> 00:04:19.033
And it was really --
it was way deeper than, like,

00:04:19.033 --> 00:04:20.067
trying to do a festival.

00:04:20.067 --> 00:04:21.901
It was an opportunity for,
like --

00:04:21.901 --> 00:04:23.501
for Black people
from around the world

00:04:23.501 --> 00:04:25.367
to get opportunity
to be together

00:04:25.367 --> 00:04:27.734
and commune safely and, like,
have some good music.

00:04:27.734 --> 00:04:29.167
It was a free concert.
-Yeah.

00:04:29.167 --> 00:04:31.701
-Dave Chappelle, Erykah Badu,
T-Pain.

00:04:31.701 --> 00:04:33.000
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.300
-That's a --
It was a big deal, man.

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:35.367
-Yeah.
-You pulled it off.

00:04:35.367 --> 00:04:36.567
-No, thank you, man.
-Would you --

00:04:36.567 --> 00:04:38.434
Would you ever do another one?
-I am.

00:04:38.434 --> 00:04:41.767
I'm actually looking
to do it again in January.

00:04:41.767 --> 00:04:44.767
Myself and Vic Mensa are looking
to do it again next year.

00:04:44.767 --> 00:04:47.834
We're actually thinking about
hosting the next one

00:04:47.834 --> 00:04:50.267
in Kingston, Jamaica.

00:04:50.267 --> 00:04:52.167
And -- And we're --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:52.167 --> 00:04:53.767
-Really?
-Yeah.

00:04:53.767 --> 00:04:55.667
We're still -- we're still
working it out in our heads.

00:04:55.667 --> 00:04:58.367
And we love the city
of Accra and --

00:04:58.367 --> 00:05:00.300
and Ghana and West Africa
as a whole.

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:04.534
And we want to just continue to,
like, create community

00:05:04.534 --> 00:05:08.133
in other spaces and, like --
and not make it such a, like,

00:05:08.133 --> 00:05:09.501
you know, stagnant thing.

00:05:09.501 --> 00:05:11.634
And so, we're just looking,
like -- for any countries

00:05:11.634 --> 00:05:14.067
that want to host us
and want to host Black folks

00:05:14.067 --> 00:05:17.367
from around the diaspora --
-Yeah.

00:05:17.367 --> 00:05:18.534
-Hit us up, yo.

00:05:18.534 --> 00:05:21.067
We want to bring that experience
around the world.

00:05:21.067 --> 00:05:22.234
-Dude, good for you.
I'm glad you're doing that.

00:05:22.234 --> 00:05:23.300
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Thanks, man.

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:27.367
-That's good
that you're doing that.

00:05:27.367 --> 00:05:29.334
Talking about your "Yah Know."
-"Yah Know."

00:05:29.334 --> 00:05:31.133
-You're performing this tonight.
If you want to see

00:05:31.133 --> 00:05:34.000
Chance perform, he's performing
later in the show.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:35.767
Who choreographs this?

00:05:35.767 --> 00:05:37.167
Is there some artist
behind this?

00:05:37.167 --> 00:05:38.467
Is there something that you --
-Yeah.

00:05:38.467 --> 00:05:40.934
So -- no, so it's like,
all of my music,

00:05:40.934 --> 00:05:43.267
and all most artists' music,
like, what we typically do

00:05:43.267 --> 00:05:46.100
as recording artists,
we get in the studio over,

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:47.934
you know, the course of a year,
a couple years,

00:05:47.934 --> 00:05:49.767
and make a bunch of songs.

00:05:49.767 --> 00:05:51.868
And when we're done,
we go and find an artist

00:05:51.868 --> 00:05:54.267
that'll make the cover art
for that album, right?

00:05:54.267 --> 00:05:55.634
-Yeah.
-And it'll be, you know,

00:05:55.634 --> 00:05:58.167
one visual piece that represents
all these different songs.

00:05:58.167 --> 00:06:00.834
And what I've been trying to do
and learning to do

00:06:00.834 --> 00:06:03.667
is collaborate
with visual artists

00:06:03.667 --> 00:06:05.634
in the process
of making my songs

00:06:05.634 --> 00:06:10.367
and having each individual song
have its own visible, like,

00:06:10.367 --> 00:06:13.067
physical, tangible piece of art
that goes along with it.

00:06:13.067 --> 00:06:14.901
And so, now I'm, like,
five songs deep,

00:06:14.901 --> 00:06:16.067
but there's, like, real --

00:06:16.067 --> 00:06:18.167
Like, this is a real painting
that exists.

00:06:18.167 --> 00:06:19.501
It was on --
It was actually on display

00:06:19.501 --> 00:06:21.834
at the Museum
of Contemporary Art, L.A.,

00:06:21.834 --> 00:06:23.467
for about a month.

00:06:23.467 --> 00:06:25.634
And a lot of paintings
that go with my songs

00:06:25.634 --> 00:06:26.834
have been kind of
traveling the world.

00:06:26.834 --> 00:06:28.834
-Who did this one?
-This is an amazing artist

00:06:28.834 --> 00:06:31.133
by the name of Mia Lee,
who's out of Chicago.

00:06:31.133 --> 00:06:33.868
She's -- She's collaborated
with a lot of different artists.

00:06:33.868 --> 00:06:35.467
She has a new collection
at Urban Outfitters.

00:06:35.467 --> 00:06:37.667
But she's somebody I grew up
with, who's just amazingly --

00:06:37.667 --> 00:06:39.734
immensely talented.
She inspires me.

00:06:39.734 --> 00:06:43.000
And -- And, yeah,
we made the record, "Yah Know,"

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:46.801
at the end of last year
and travelled together to Ghana

00:06:46.801 --> 00:06:49.434
and kind of just, like...
I don't know.

00:06:49.434 --> 00:06:51.667
Just, like, taught each other
a lot of things.

00:06:51.667 --> 00:06:54.400
And it came out of this -- this
conversation and collaboration,

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:57.033
that this, like, crazy song
with a lot of meaning

00:06:57.033 --> 00:06:58.868
and a lot of history came to be.

00:06:58.868 --> 00:07:00.267
-Well, good for you
for breaking new talent.

00:07:00.267 --> 00:07:01.968
I like that.
You know, I think that's cool.

00:07:01.968 --> 00:07:04.767
[ Cheers and applause ]
I feel like --

00:07:04.767 --> 00:07:07.334
I feel like -- you know,
"The Voice" is the lead-in

00:07:07.334 --> 00:07:09.434
for "That's My Jam"
tomorrow night.

00:07:09.434 --> 00:07:10.567
For me,
I'm very excited about this.

00:07:10.567 --> 00:07:12.167
-Yeah.
-And you were nice enough to --

00:07:12.167 --> 00:07:13.367
to be on "That's My Jam."

00:07:13.367 --> 00:07:15.834
You sang a country version
of "Hot in Herre."

00:07:15.834 --> 00:07:18.701
-Yeah, I did.
-And it was a massive --

00:07:18.701 --> 00:07:19.968
It was amazing.
I mean, it went viral.

00:07:19.968 --> 00:07:21.334
It was crazy.

00:07:21.334 --> 00:07:24.167
I think that it might have
started your country career.

00:07:24.167 --> 00:07:25.434
-Mm.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:25.434 --> 00:07:28.901
-Would you ever consider
doing a country song?

00:07:28.901 --> 00:07:30.033
-Maybe.
You know what?

00:07:30.033 --> 00:07:31.434
I --
[ Laughter ]

00:07:31.434 --> 00:07:33.033
-You just -- you just shook
your head and said, "Maybe."

00:07:33.033 --> 00:07:34.167
-No, I mean, I will --
-You're like, "Yeah,

00:07:34.167 --> 00:07:35.667
I would probably think about it,
Jimmy."

00:07:35.667 --> 00:07:37.000
-My brain is moving fast.
You know what?

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:40.901
I would do it if I could, like,
do it with, like, the --

00:07:40.901 --> 00:07:44.200
You know, who I consider to be
the most important country star

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:47.300
of our time?
The incomparable Nelly.

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:49.167
-Blake Shelton -- oh.
I thought you were gonna -- no.

00:07:49.167 --> 00:07:50.400
[ Laughter ]
That's what I meant.

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:52.133
I meant Nelly.
I meant -- I meant Nelly.

00:07:52.133 --> 00:07:53.567
Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-No, Blake is actually

00:07:53.567 --> 00:07:54.901
watching this right now.

00:07:54.901 --> 00:07:56.634
And I was setting it up
so he would feel that.

00:07:56.634 --> 00:07:58.501
But, no, I'm talking about
Nelly, man!

00:07:58.501 --> 00:07:59.734
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:59.734 --> 00:08:02.100
St. Louis is on!
-You would do it with Nelly?

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:03.734
-Yeah.
No, he's an amazing artist.

00:08:03.734 --> 00:08:05.701
I remember I used to listen
to -- I mean, he made

00:08:05.701 --> 00:08:08.167
"Hot in Herre," which, to me,
is naturally a country song.

00:08:08.167 --> 00:08:10.901
But also, like --
What's the one?

00:08:10.901 --> 00:08:13.868
♪ And it's all in my head,
I think about it over ♪

00:08:13.868 --> 00:08:15.968
Like, I -- he's got --
he's got some country classics.

00:08:15.968 --> 00:08:17.367
Nelly's a legend.
-All right. Nelly, if --

00:08:17.367 --> 00:08:19.100
Reach out if you want to do
a country song with Chance.

00:08:19.100 --> 00:08:20.601
-Yeah, I would do that
in a second.

00:08:20.601 --> 00:08:22.634
-How -- How --
